     7	## 0. Formulation, alphabet and monomial order
     8	
     9	**Book problem (3).** Give Gröbner–Shirshov (GS) bases of the finite-type Artin groups in the
    10	Artin–Garside generators $\sigma_i,\Delta,\Delta^{-1}$ (book p. 33; type $A$ was done by Bokut,
    11	arXiv:0806.1125, J. Symbolic Comput. 43 (2008) 397–405).
    12	
    13	**Setting.** $(W,S)$ is a finite Coxeter system, $S=\{s_1,\dots,s_n\}$, $m_{st}$ the Coxeter
    14	matrix, $N=\ell(w_0)$ the number of positive roots. $A=A(W)$ is the Artin group and $A^+$ the
    15	Artin monoid, generated by (lifts of) $S$ with relations $\Pi_{m_{st}}(s,t)=\Pi_{m_{st}}(t,s)$,
    16	where $\Pi_k(s,t)=sts\cdots$ ($k$ letters). $\Delta\in A^+$ is the lift of $w_0$ and
    17	$\varphi(s)=w_0sw_0$. The alphabet is the book's exact Artin–Garside alphabet
    18	$$X=S\cup\{\Delta,\Delta^{-1}\},$$
    19	and $A$ is presented as a *semigroup* on $X$ by the braid relations, $\Delta=w_\Delta$ (any
    20	positive word for $\Delta$), $\Delta\Delta^{-1}=1=\Delta^{-1}\Delta$ (every $s$ is then
    21	invertible: $s\cdot(s^{-1}\Delta)\Delta^{-1}=1$ with $s^{-1}\Delta\in A^+$).
    22	
    23	**Monomial order (a choice, disclosed).** The book does not fix a monomial order. Throughout we
    24	use the deg-lex order on $X^*$ induced by a letter order
    25	$$\Delta^{-1}<\Delta<s_1<\dots<s_n,$$
    26	where the order of $S$ is arbitrary (it is part of the data; results depend on it). This is
    27	exactly Bokut's convention for type $A$. Nothing below is claimed for other monomial orders.
    28	
    29	**Not this problem.** Convergent (quadratic) rewriting systems for Garside/Artin monoids on the
    30	alphabet of *all simple elements* (Garside generators) are a different formulation on a different
    31	alphabet (see README, State of the art). Here the alphabet has $n+2$ letters.
    32	
    33	Letters in examples and code: generator $k$ of the chosen order is `a`,`b`,`c`,…; `1`$=\Delta$,
    34	`0`$=\Delta^{-1}$.
    35	
    36	## 1. Background facts used (standard, not reproved)
    37	
    38	* (G1) $A^+$ is cancellative and embeds in $A$ (Brieskorn–Saito 1972; Deligne 1972).
    39	* (G2) Any two elements of $A^+$ have a left lcm $a\vee b$ (least common right multiple w.r.t.
    40	  left divisibility $\preceq$); for atoms $s\ne t$, $s\vee t=\Pi_{m_{st}}(s,t)=\Pi_{m_{st}}(t,s)$
    41	  (Brieskorn–Saito).
    42	* (G3) The simple elements (left divisors of $\Delta$) are the positive lifts of the elements of
    43	  $W$; for simples, $\preceq$ is the right weak order of $W$; $s\preceq g$ for $g$ simple iff
    44	  $s$ is a left descent of $g$.
    45	* (G4) $s\Delta=\Delta\varphi(s)$ for $s\in S$; $\varphi$ is an involutive diagram automorphism.
    46	* (G5) Every $g\in A$ is uniquely $g=\Delta^k p$ with $k\in\mathbb Z$, $p\in A^+$, $\Delta\not\preceq p$
    47	  (Garside 1969; Brieskorn–Saito).
    48	* (G6) $M(t):=\sum_{p\in A^+}t^{\ell(p)}=1/D(t)$, $D(t)=\sum_{T\subseteq S}(-1)^{|T|}t^{N_T}$, $N_T=\ell(w_0(W_T))$
    49	  (Deligne/Saito). Short proof: for $p\ne1$, $\sum_{T\subseteq L(p)}(-1)^{|T|}=0$ where
    50	  $L(p)=\{s:s\preceq p\}$; $T\subseteq L(p)\iff\Delta_T\preceq p$ by (G2); summing
    51	  $t^{\ell(p)}$ and writing $p=\Delta_Tq$ (G1) gives $D(t)M(t)=1$.
    52	
    53	For $a,b\in A^+$ write $a\backslash b:=a^{-1}(a\vee b)$ (right complement). Then
    54	$$b\preceq ah\iff a\backslash b\preceq h,\qquad (gh)\backslash c=h\backslash(g\backslash c),\qquad c\preceq c'\Rightarrow a\backslash c\preceq a\backslash c'.\tag{1.1}$$
    55	For atoms $x\ne t$: $x\backslash t=\Pi_{m_{tx}-1}(t,x)$ (alternating word of length
    56	$m_{tx}-1$ beginning with $t$). If $c$ is simple then $y\backslash c\preceq y^{-1}\Delta$ is simple.
    57	
    58	## 2. GS bases of semigroup presentations: the criterion we use
    59	
    60	Let a semigroup $G$ be presented on a finite alphabet $X$, $<$ a monomial well order, and
    61	$\mathrm{NF}\subset X^*$ the set of $<$-least representatives. Let $S$ be a set of pairs $(u,v)$
    62	with $u=v$ in $G$ and $v<u$ (leading word $\bar u=u$).
    63	
    64	**Lemma 1.** (a) $\mathrm{NF}\subseteq\mathrm{Irr}(S)$. (b) The following are equivalent:
    65	(i) $S$ is a GS basis (all compositions trivial) and $\langle X\mid S\rangle\to G$ is an isomorphism;
    66	(ii) $\mathrm{Irr}(S)=\mathrm{NF}$; (iii) every word $u\notin\mathrm{NF}$ all of whose proper factors
    67	lie in NF is a leading word of $S$; (iv) $|\mathrm{Irr}(S)\cap X^L|=|\mathrm{NF}\cap X^L|$ for all $L$.
    68	(c) Consequently the *reduced* GS basis is $\{u\to\mathrm{NF}(u): u\in\mathrm{MinRed}\}$,
    69	$\mathrm{MinRed}$ the set of words $u\notin\mathrm{NF}$ whose proper factors are all in NF, and
    70	**every** GS basis of $G$ on $X$ for $<$ has at least $|\mathrm{MinRed}|$ elements.
    71	
    72	*Proof.* (a) If $w\in\mathrm{NF}$ contained a leading word $u$, replacing it by $v<u$ gives a
    73	smaller word for the same element. (ii)⇒(i): $\mathrm{Irr}(S)$ spans $k\langle X|S\rangle$ (every word
    74	reduces to an irreducible one), and its image in $k[G]$ is a set of distinct group elements,
    75	hence linearly independent; by the Composition–Diamond lemma (Shirshov 1962; Bokut 1976;
    76	Bergman 1978) $S$ is a GS basis, and two words equal in $G$ reduce to the same irreducible word,
    77	so the congruences of $S$ and of $G$ coincide. (i)⇒(ii): by the CD lemma $\mathrm{Irr}(S)$ is a basis
    78	of $k\langle X|S\rangle=k[G]$, so distinct irreducible words are distinct elements; with (a) and the
    79	fact that each element has a least word, $\mathrm{Irr}(S)=\mathrm{NF}$. (ii)⇔(iii): NF is closed
    80	under factors; a word outside NF contains a minimal non-normal factor, which by (iii) is a leading
    81	word; conversely if $u\in\mathrm{MinRed}$ and $\mathrm{Irr}(S)=\mathrm{NF}$ then $u$ contains a
    82	leading word, and proper factors of $u$ are normal hence irreducible, so the leading word is $u$.
    83	(iv)⇔(ii) by (a). (c) follows from (iii). ∎
    84	
    85	## 3. Normal forms on the Artin–Garside alphabet
    86	
    87	**Lemma 2.** Assume $N\ge2$. For $g=\Delta^kp$ as in (G5) the geodesic length of $g$ over $X$ is
    88	$|k|+\ell(p)$, and the deg-lex normal form is
    89	$$\mathrm{NF}(g)=\Delta^{(k)}\,\mathrm{lexmin}(p),\qquad \Delta^{(k)}=\Delta^k\ (k\ge0),\ (\Delta^{-1})^{|k|}\ (k<0),$$
    90	where $\mathrm{lexmin}(p)$ is the deg-lex least positive word of $p$. Hence the growth series of
    91	$A$ over $X$ is $G(t)=(1-t^N)M(t)\frac{1+t}{1-t}$.
    92	
    93	*Proof.* Let $w$ represent $g$ with $e_\pm$ letters $\Delta^{\pm1}$ and positive part $P$. By
    94	(G4) $w=\Delta^{m}P'$ in $A$ with $m=e_+-e_-$ and $P'$ positive, $|P'|=|P|$. Then
    95	$P'=\Delta^{k-m}p$ with $P'\in A^+$; if $k<m$ then $p=\Delta^{m-k}P'$ would be divisible by
    96	$\Delta$, so $k\ge m$ and $|P'|=(k-m)N+\ell(p)$ (homogeneity). Thus
    97	$|w|\ge|m|+(k-m)N+\ell(p)\ge|k|+\ell(p)$, with equality only if $m=k$ (as $N\ge2$) and
    98	$e_++e_-=|m|$. So geodesics consist of $|k|$ letters $\Delta^{\operatorname{sgn}k}$ and a positive
    99	part of length $\ell(p)$; since $\Delta^{\pm1}$ are the smallest letters, the lex-least one puts
   100	all of them first, followed by the least positive word of $p$. The count: elements of length $L$
   101	are pairs $(k,p)$ with $|k|+\ell(p)=L$; $\sum_{p\ \Delta\text{-free}}t^{\ell(p)}=(1-t^N)M(t)$ by
   102	(G5) applied in $A^+$. ∎
   103	
   104	Call a positive word *normal* if it is lexmin and its element is not divisible by $\Delta$.
   105	
   106	## 4. Theorem A (structure of the reduced GS basis) — PROVED
   107	
   108	**Theorem A.** For every finite $W$ with $N\ge2$ and every order of $S$, the reduced GS basis of
   109	$A(W)$ on $X$ (deg-lex, $\Delta^{-1}<\Delta<S$) is the disjoint union of
   110	* $I=\{\Delta\Delta^{-1}\to1,\ \Delta^{-1}\Delta\to1\}$,
   111	* $T=\{s\Delta\to\Delta\varphi(s),\ s\Delta^{-1}\to\Delta^{-1}\varphi(s): s\in S\}$,
   112	* $P=\{u\to\mathrm{NF}(u): u\in S^+\text{ positive},\ u\text{ not normal},\ \text{all proper factors of }u\text{ normal}\}$.
   113	
   114	For $u\in P$: $\mathrm{NF}(u)=\mathrm{lexmin}([u])$ if $[u]$ is $\Delta$-free, and otherwise
   115	$\mathrm{NF}(u)=\Delta\,\mathrm{lexmin}(\Delta^{-1}[u])$ with $\Delta^{-1}[u]$ $\Delta$-free.
   116	
   117	*Proof.* By Lemma 1(c) we must determine MinRed. By Lemma 2 the words in NF are exactly
   118	$\Delta^{(k)}w$ with $w$ a normal positive word. Let $u\in\mathrm{MinRed}$ contain a letter
   119	$\Delta^{\pm1}$. If $u$ has a factor $\Delta\Delta^{-1}$ or $\Delta^{-1}\Delta$ (not normal: it
   120	represents $1$) or $s\Delta^{\pm1}$ (not normal: its normal form is $\Delta^{\pm1}\varphi(s)$ by
   121	(G4) and Lemma 2), minimality forces $u$ to be that factor. Otherwise all $\Delta$-letters of $u$
   122	have the same sign and precede all positive letters, i.e. $u=\Delta^{(k)}w$ with $k\ne0$ and $w$
   123	positive; since $u\notin\mathrm{NF}$, Lemma 2 shows that $w$ is not normal, but $w$ is a proper
   124	factor of $u$ — contradiction. Hence the non-positive part of MinRed is $I\cup T$ (each of these
   125	words is non-normal with normal proper factors), and the positive part is $P$ by definition.
   126	For $u=xv\in P$ ($x\in S$) the factor $v$ is normal. If $\Delta^2\preceq[u]=x[v]$ then by (1.1)
   127	$x^{-1}\Delta^2=x\backslash\Delta^2\preceq[v]$; but $x^{-1}\Delta\cdot\Delta=\Delta\cdot\varphi(x^{-1}\Delta)$ by (G4),
   128	so $\Delta\preceq[v]$, contradicting normality of $v$. Thus $[u]=\Delta^kp$ with $k\in\{0,1\}$ and
   129	Lemma 2 gives the stated normal form. ∎
   130	
   131	**Lemma 3 (parabolic closure).** For $J\subsetneq S$ and $u\in J^*$: $u$ is normal iff $u$ is the
   132	deg-lex least word of its element in the parabolic monoid $A^+_J$. Hence
   133	$P\cap J^*=\mathrm{MinRed}(A_J^+)$, the leading words of the reduced deg-lex GS basis of the
   134	*monoid* $A^+_J$ with the induced order.
   135	
   136	*Proof.* Braid relations preserve the set of letters occurring in a word, so every word of
   137	$[u]$ lies in $J^*$; lexmin in $A^+$ and in $A^+_J$ coincide. An element of $A^+_J$ is never
   138	divisible by $\Delta$ when $J\ne S$ (every word for $\Delta$ contains all letters of $S$). ∎
   139	
   140	## 5. Lemma 4 and Theorem B (finite-automaton description, all finite types) — PROVED
   141	
   142	**Lemma 4.** A positive word $v=v_1\cdots v_L$ is normal iff for every position $k$:
   143	(L) $\Pi_{m-1}(t,v_k)\not\preceq[v_{k+1}\cdots v_L]$ for every $t<v_k$ with $m=m_{tv_k}$, and
   144	(D) $v_k^{-1}\Delta\not\preceq[v_{k+1}\cdots v_L]$.
   145	Consequently $u=xv\in P$ ($x\in S$) iff $v$ and $xv'$ are normal and
   146	$\Pi_{m_{tx}-1}(t,x)\preceq[v]$ for some $t<x$, or $x^{-1}\Delta\preceq[v]$.
   147	
   148	*Proof.* $\mathrm{lexmin}(g)=s\cdot\mathrm{lexmin}(s^{-1}g)$ with $s=\min L(g)$, so $v$ is lexmin iff
   149	$v_k=\min L([v_k\cdots v_L])$ for every $k$, iff no $t<v_k$ divides $[v_k\cdots v_L]$; by (1.1)
   150	$t\preceq v_kh\iff v_k\backslash t=\Pi_{m-1}(t,v_k)\preceq h$. $\Delta$-freeness of $[v]$ is
   151	inherited by all factors (if $[w]=\Delta w'$ then $[pws]=\Delta\varphi([p])w'[s]$ by (G4)), and
   152	$\Delta\preceq v_kh\iff v_k^{-1}\Delta\preceq h$. The second statement is the first applied to
   153	$xv'$, $v$ and $xv$. ∎
   154	
   155	**Theorem B.** For every finite $W$ with $N\ge2$ (i.e. not of type $A_1$, which Theorem C treats
   156	separately) and every order of $S$, the set of leading words of the reduced GS basis is a regular
   157	language, recognised by an explicit finite automaton built from $W$:
   158	states are antichains (w.r.t. $\preceq$) of simple elements ("obligations"); reading $y\in S$ maps
   159	the state $O$ to the set of minimal elements of
   160	$\{y\backslash c: c\in O\}\cup\{\Pi_{m_{ty}-1}(t,y): t<y\}\cup\{y^{-1}\Delta\}$, and the word dies
   161	if some $y\backslash c=1$. A positive word is normal iff its run from $\emptyset$ never dies;
   162	$P$-leading words are $u$ with $u_{[1,L-1]}$ and $u_{[2,L]}$ normal and $u$ not normal
   163	(pair automaton). Together with $I\cup T$ this describes the reduced GS basis for every finite type.
   164	
   165	*Proof.* Induction on $k$ with the invariant: after reading $v_1\cdots v_k$ the state is (the minimal
   166	elements of) $\{[v_{j+1}\cdots v_k]\backslash c\}$ over all obligations $c$ created at positions
   167	$j\le k$, by (1.1) ($c\not\preceq[v_{j+1}\cdots v_kh]\iff[v_{j+1}\cdots v_k]\backslash c\not\preceq h$
