   462	The book asks for a GS basis; the stronger reduced-basis problem is distinguished below.
   463	Only the alphabet $S\cup\{\Delta,\Delta^{-1}\}$ is used as algebra generators. Simple
   464	elements occur as finite *indices*, never as additional algebra generators.
   465	
   466	Fix $N\ge2$, a total order on $S$, and for each $w\in W$ a reduced Coxeter word $\rho(w)$,
   467	with $\rho(1)=\epsilon$. For definiteness choose the lex-least reduced Coxeter word: repeatedly
   468	remove the least left descent. This is a computation in the finite group $W$, not an
   469	Artin-group normal-form oracle. The positive lift of $w$ is independent of the chosen
   470	reduced word by Matsumoto's theorem. For simples $u,v$, use the finite weak-order join to define
   471	$u\backslash v=u^{-1}(u\vee v)\in W$; its positive lift is the right complement in (1.1).
   472	
   473	**Theorem G (uniform rational GS basis).** For every finite Coxeter system and every order of
   474	its atoms, add the following rules to the twists and cancellations of Theorem A. Choose an
   475	atom $x$ and a target $q$, where either $q=t\in S$ with $t<x$, or $q=\Delta$ (indexed by $w_0$).
   476	Set $e_0=x\backslash q$ and, for a nonempty atom word $v=y_1\cdots y_k$, set
   477	$$e_i=y_i\backslash e_{i-1},\qquad f_i=e_{i-1}\backslash y_i\quad(1\le i\le k).$$
   478	For **every** such sequence satisfying $e_i\ne1$ for $0\le i<k$ and $e_k=1$, include
   479	$$
   480	 \boxed{\quad x\,y_1\cdots y_k\ \longrightarrow\
   481	          q\,\rho(q\backslash x)\,\rho(f_1)\cdots\rho(f_k).\quad} \tag{11.1}
   482	$$
   483	Here the first $q$ on the right is the single letter $t$ or $\Delta$, not a word for $w_0$.
   484	All complement calculations use only the finite weak-order table of $W$. Formula (11.1),
   485	including its right side, is therefore an explicit finite-state family with arbitrary-length
   486	paths; it does not mean "$u\to\mathrm{NF}(u)$" and does not invoke an Artin normal form.
   487	
   488	The resulting monic binomials form a GS basis over any field for deg-lex with
